# `loop-reads-then-writes` with its body in a step of its own: `show` may write the note, then reads it, and the loop runs
# `show` again. What a route does at a place composes from what its steps do there (the effect functor), so the route is
# the one body written out: the first round reads the note as it was and a later round writes over it. Read, then
# written: both.

route loopfill.run
step run/for word in sys.argv[1:]                           # the words the human typed, each kept as `word`
step run/show
line entry > run call
line entry > run/for word in sys.argv[1:] call             # the command line is the entry's
line run/for word in sys.argv[1:] > _·word@run/for word in sys.argv[1:] value
line _·word@run/for word in sys.argv[1:] > run/show value   # `show(word)`
home _·word@run/for word in sys.argv[1:] run
line File(data/note.txt) > run/show own
line run/show > File(data/note.txt) own
line run/show > Screen own

side entry left
side File(data/note.txt) both
side Screen right
